# Penny Lane, verse (figure 104.1)
# Eight bars, two to a system. The "walking bass cliche" B A# G# F# runs in quarter notes under the
# B chord in bars 1 and 3; in bar 2 the bass takes the roots E C# F#, with the F# repeated an
# octave apart on beat 4 (Pollack's "F#8").
# The F# chord in bar 2 lasts beats 3-4 (bass F# F#). Bar 4's bass is a whole-note B under b7.
# Pollack types no bass for bars 5-8, so those systems have chords only.
# The asterisks mark the bars whose harmony he calls "extremely chromatic" (bars 4 and 5); kept as
# the annotation under b7 and g#.
# Bars 7-8: the 4-3 suspension over F#, resolving on beat 3 of bar 8.
#? "F#8": read as the F# jumping up an octave (F#2 to F#3) on beat 4 of bar 2; it may be meant as an octave down instead.
key B

system m. 1
chords "Chords"     | B{I} | E{IV} c#{ii} F#{V}*2 |
bass "Bassline"     | B2 A#2 G#2 F#2 | E2 C#2 F#2 F#3 |

system m. 3
chords "Chords"     | B{I} | b7{* * * * * *} |
bass "Bassline"     | B2 A#2 G#2 F#2 | B2 |

system m. 5
chords "Chords"     | g#{* * * * * *} | G{flat-VI} |

system m. 7
chords "Chords"     | F#{V4} | -*2 -{3}*2 |
